  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Monday: The slow moving low continues to move \nover the region. Precipitable water amounts continue to rise to\n0.8-1.0 by Monday. The increasing moisture will bring a steady \nstream of clouds across the region. As the low moves east, the \nmoisture swath shifts away from the Cascades and more over the\nIdaho Panhandle and eastern Washington. For Sunday, precip \nchances start mainly over the Idaho Panhandle and expands west \nthrough the afternoon and evening hours. Precip chances rise to \n60-90 percent over southeast/east-central WA and central and \nsouthern ID Panhandle from Sunday evening through Monday \nmorning. 24-hour probabilities from 5 PM Sunday to 5 PM Monday \nfor 0.50 inches are between 70-90 percent, 60-80 percent for \n0.75 inches and 30-60 percent for 1 inch.\n\nAfternoon and evening instability will lead to weak \nthunderstorm chances. Cape values are in the in the 400-800 J/kg\nrange with moderate lapse rates support weak convection. The \nmain threat with the thunderstorms will be lightning and gusty \noutflow winds. Winds will generally be light outside of the \nthunderstorms. Monday afternoon will be breezy with gusts near \n25 mph across the Basin. Highs will be in 70s to low 80s. Lows \nwill also be mild with a range of 40s to low 50s.\n\nTuesday through Friday: The ensembles are in decent agreement \nfor the extended. A flat ridge pattern will set up over the \nregion as the Low exits. It will lead to dry, warming trend \nthrough the end of the week. Precip chances will be less than 10\npercent through Thursday. Another trough will drop in from BC \nFriday as remnants of tropical system begin to press moisture \ninto the Pacific Northwest. It is leading to increasing precip \nchances going into the weekend. NBM thunderstorm potential in \naround 10-20 percent across the Inland Northwest with highest \npercents over Northeast WA and Idaho Panhandle late Friday and \nSaturday. It is too early to have much confidence in the \nupcoming weekend weather but something to keep on the radar. \nHighs for the latter half of the week will be in the 80s. \nOvernight lows will be in the upper 40s and 50s. /JDC",
